ur-month commitment  most from the third battalion of  princess patricia's canadian light  infantry in edmonton some are already  here and getting ready to start it's a  pretty profound thing when you think  about it  so what it means for us is it's really  important to deliver hard realistic  training for the short time we have them  and  and i guess prepare them for what lies  ahead this will be a resumption of  canada's operation unifier training  mission but now with much higher stakes  it was paused when russia invaded  ukraine in february over the previous  seven years canadian forces had  instructed 33 000 ukrainian soldiers and  security personnel canadian special  forces are also on the ground in ukraine  conducting training exercises there too  redmond shannon global news in the  southeast of england  one month after russia announced it  would be pulling its support of the  international space station a model of  the country's planned replacement has  been unveiled  russia says it will quit the iss at the  end of 2024 in favor of its own orbital  station russian state media says the  first component for the new station  could be launched as early as 2025 and  completed by 2035.  more raging wildfires in europe ahead  the inferno in spain forcing hundreds to  flee  more than 1200 families have been forced  from their homes due to a trio of fierce  and fast-moving wildfires in spain  gusty winds and difficult terrain is  making for challenging firefighting  conditions forcing crews to rely on  planes and helicopters to attack the  flames drought and long stretches of  extreme temperatures have made 2022 the  worst fire year of the century with  spain recording 43 major fires so far  four times the number last year kenya's  deputy president william rudow has won  the country's fiercely contested  presidential election  these are the chaotic scenes inside the  maine tallying center as the result was  announced rudol narrowly beat his main  opponent veteran opposition leader raila  odinga four of the seven members of the  electoral commission rejected the result  claiming the vote was rigged violent  protests erupted and police fired tear  gas to disperse the crowd of bodinga  supporters now kenyans are waiting to  see if odinga will contest the election  results  at least 18 children are among dozens  killed when an electrical fire swept  through a coptic church in egypt on  sunday funerals are being held for the  victims the children were aged 3 to 16.  thousands of people were attending mass  at the time officials say most of the  deaths and injuries were caused by smoke  inhalation but the panic also triggered  a stampede  it's believed the fire started on the  second floor when an air conditioner  malfunctioned building repairs are now  underway  next a family who raised millions to  find a miracle for their son's  ultra-rare disease  a few years ago we first told you about  a young toronto boy with a devastating  diagnosis  michael pirovalakis suffers from a  debilitating neurodegenerative disease  so rare he's believed to be the only  child in canada afflicted with it now  for the first time ever he's undergoing  an experimental gene therapy  specifically designed for him  that after his family raised millions to  make that treatment possible  karen lieberman has more  mama's hand yeah  you're scared that you know you're doing  something the wrong thing that you might  harm  you know this beautiful little boy but  the same time you're like you know this  could change his life it still seems  like unbelievable it like i can't  believe  we made it to that point three years  after michael pirovalakis was diagnosed  with the ultra rare condition [ __ ]  paraplegia 50 known as spg50 he became  the first to receive gene therapy with  the goal of treating it i think there  was about a hundred scientists involved  in this journey michael's parents raised  more than three million dollars to  finance the research and development of  a specialized therapy to save their son  and potentially find a cure where none  currently exists first of all we took  our life savings to start the programs  because we couldn't wait you know we had  beer stands beer markets galas soccer  tournaments  people doing little kids doing lemonade  stands on the side of the street in  march after successful testing in  animals and health canada approval the  moment they'd worked tirelessly toward a  team at toronto's sick kids hospital  delivered the individualized gene  therapy in a single patient trial to  michael and the idea behind gene therapy  is you basically replace the gene so  that now this missing protein can be  made and the idea being well hopefully  that should now  either halt whatever is happening with  the disease and maybe even reverse  things beyond helping michael and others  with rare conditions doctors believe  this could be a springboard to develop  treatments for more common conditions  you can apply the same principles of  from fundamental discovery to the  development of the drug to give to the  patient  across the continuum of disease  focus he's watching your feet michael  now undergoes daily therapy while his  family waits to see if the treatment  will work so no one really knows it's  uncharted territory you know all we're  trying to do is give him just a better  life oh my gosh  and although for michael it may just be  a treatment for the newer generation it  will be a cure right  and so  it's just like an amazing feeling  to know we're helping other people too  karen lieberman global news toronto  that is it for global national for this  monday night i'm farah nasser and on  behalf of our whole team here at global  national i want to thank you so much for  spending part of your evening with us  tonight's your canada is the city of  niagara falls ontario we love seeing  your canada so please keep emailing us  those photos to viewers at  globalnational.com  until tomorrow take care 